{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED at exact head 4dcd5fe900778d0a262c50facff738023fc56171, against request d7c81f7a and all ten exact-path artifacts.\n\nMost of this head is ready. It builds, vets, and passes both the plain and the -race suites; I followed the README first-user path verbatim in a clean worktree and every command worked exactly as written, including the shared-secret invitation flow and the removal of the stray mkdir. Named games, board squares and tooltips, the help dialog, and the retirement of the old copy are all in place and pinned by tests. One blocking defect stops approval, and it is in the very feature the request calls out: reason-bearing invalid-move behaviour.\n\nBLOCKING: A PROMOTION IS REFUSED WITH A FACTUALLY FALSE REASON.\n\nchess.go, in foldMove's MoveStr error path. When MoveStr fails, the code asks LegalSelection for a reason; when that reason is empty — which is exactly the case where the source square DOES have legal destinations — it falls to the else branch and asserts \"destination %s is not valid from %s\", without ever checking whether the destination is in the list it just computed. Promotion destinations are stored with their promotion suffix in LegalSelection (\"to += move.Promo().String()\", giving b8q, b8r, b8b, b8n), so a bare four-character b8 never matches and the false branch always fires for a promoting pawn.\n\nThe result: a7b8, a legal promotion capture typed without the promotion piece, is refused with \"move is illegal in the current position: destination b8 is not valid from a7\". b8 IS a valid destination. The message contradicts the fold's own computed destination list, and it points the player away from the move that would work while never mentioning the actual fault. a7a8 gets the same misleading text.\n\nTwo things make this blocking rather than cosmetic. First, the refusal reason is written permanently into the durable log, so a wrong reason is not a transient message but a false record. Second, the audience is a first-time user, and README.md's UCI paragraph (\"e2e4 means move from e2 to e4\") never mentions that a promotion needs a fifth character — so the one place that could teach them stays silent and the error message actively misleads them. The information needed to say the right thing is already in hand at that point in the function.\n\nThe fix is small and local: before the else, test whether any destination in the selection begins with body.Move[2:4]; if one does, say the move needs a promotion piece and name an example such as a7b8q. Please also add the promotion clause to the README's UCI sentence in the same head, since the two halves of this defect only close together.\n\nNON-BLOCKING, for your judgement, not conditions of satisfaction: app.css still defines .eyebrow, which no template uses since this head replaced it with .breadcrumb, while game.html still carries the class game-shell whose rule this head deleted — dead in both directions. The game page tells the player to ask their agent to use the MCP join tool, and neither the dialog nor the README shows how to register the MCP server, so the instruction is unfollowable as written by its stated audience; a short config snippet would close it. The live panel's resize is effectively shrink-only, because max-width 100% resolves against the grid track it sits in, so the handle cannot widen it past that track. And any durable move calls location.reload(), which discards a manual resize and also destroys the in-tab credential, silently dropping the player from presence and chat until they rejoin — that one predates this head, but it undercuts the resizable-chat work being reviewed and deserves its own request.\n\nARCHITECTURE: preserved. This repository has no docs/ tree and no architecture reference page, so there is no contract page to check against or to update; I say that explicitly rather than passing the conclusion by default. The layers this head touches are the fold and durable vocabulary in chess.go, the read and query surface in cmd/chess/web.go and the MCP legal_destinations tool, the browser presentation layer under cmd/chess/ui, and the CLI. Every change is additive or backward-compatible: CreatePayload.Name is omitempty so records written before this head fold identically, Create survives as a thin wrapper over CreateNamed so no caller breaks, and LegalDestinations keeps its signature. The browser gained no rules engine and no durable write path — it still asks the fold what is legal. The one contract-adjacent change is the refusal reason string itself, which is display copy asserted only in tests, and that is precisely what this verdict is about.\n\nSECURITY: examined, no defect found. The new untrusted input is the game name, which reaches an HTML text node, a title element, and JSON on stdout; html/template escapes both HTML and the RCDATA title boundary, and encoding/json escapes control bytes, so neither markup injection nor terminal-escape injection through CLI output is reachable. Bounds are enforced twice, at the CreateNamed constructor and again in foldCreate, so a hand-crafted record cannot bypass them: non-empty, 256 bytes, single line. No user-controlled path reaches the filesystem — serveEmbedded binds one fixed embed path per route at construction, and traversal attempts return 404 without leaking content. boundedQuery refuses unknown, repeated, and oversize fields and validates the square before the fold is touched. Move legality is enforced server-side only. Identity, seat, and authority decisions are untouched by this head and still derive the role from the credential fingerprint rather than from any client-supplied value. The CSP is unchanged and still strict, and the new dialog's method=\"dialog\" close button works under form-action 'none'. The race detector is green on both packages, and each request opens its own projection, so no engine pointer is shared across goroutines. The residual observation, below the bar for a change request: the name validator permits tabs and bidirectional control characters, so a name can render misleadingly in the lobby without crossing a trust boundary.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION: three cuts, none of which weaken any condition of satisfaction. The aspect-ratio on .square is redundant now that .board is itself aspect-ratio 1 with eight equal rows and columns — the cell is square by construction, and keeping both only creates a way for them to disagree; keep the row/column fix, which is the part that did the work. LegalSelectionResult repeats \"Destinations: []string{}\" across four of five returns, which a single named zero value or one exit point would remove. And colorName reimplements what rules.Color already offers; if its String() does not give \"white\" and \"black\", a two-entry map literal reads better than a function.\n\nMETHOD, so you can judge the weight of this verdict. Gates run at the exact head in a clean detached worktree, Go 1.27.0 darwin/arm64: go build ./... exit 0; go vet ./... exit 0; go test -count=1 ./... ok (18.674s root, 16.431s cmd/chess); go test -race -count=1 ./... ok (29.771s, 25.049s). There is no Makefile in this repository, so those are the fallback commands rather than a project test target. I built and drove the binary through init, create, join, move, board, serve and mcp, exercised the HTTP endpoints directly, and confirmed over stdio JSON-RPC that the MCP join tool really seats a player. The blocking defect was reproduced end to end by playing a real game to a promotion and then verified a second time by reading foldMove and LegalSelection at the exact head rather than trusting the reproduction alone. I could not capture rendered screenshots: app.js long-polls forever, so headless Chrome never settles. The layout and resize findings therefore come from the CSS and the served DOM, not from dragging a handle in a browser, and I have not visually confirmed the rendered layout. One stated condition, exact copy as specified, I record as not verifiable rather than met: I was not given the copy specification, so I can only say the copy is internally consistent and that the retired strings are gone from both the templates and the tests.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:1b96687964cbba5a6089c526d7136b32ba9488a5#git:sha1:5f83ae401336a7a88770ec81604dcc2475237fa0","head":"4dcd5fe900778d0a262c50facff738023fc56171","verdict":"changes-requested"}}